{
  "root_cause": "The focus trap's delayInitialFocus option defaults to true, deferring initial focus until after the current event loop, so the assertion checking focus immediately after opening the dialog fails.",
  "repair_strategy": "Disable the delayInitialFocus option when activating the trap so that initial focus occurs synchronously with dialog opening.",
  "edits": [
    {
      "path": "index.html",
      "sha256": "85fb0980102bd0ea76fa350b8e2df6695a5bf878b5526a6a22d84ca1b8547611",
      "before": "document.getElementById('open').addEventListener('click', () => {\n  backdrop.hidden = false;\n  trap.activate();\n});",
      "after": "document.getElementById('open').addEventListener('click', () => {\n  backdrop.hidden = false;\n  trap.activate({ delayInitialFocus: false });\n});"
  }
],
  "expected_accessibility_effect": "Keyboard users will now receive immediate focus on the first focusable element (street input) when the dialog opens, satisfying WCAG 2.4.3 (focus order) and 2.1.1 (keyboard).",
  "functional_risks": [
    "Changing the timing of focus initialization could affect scenarios that rely on the delayed focus (e.g., if the dialog content is not yet ready). In this case the dialog is static and ready immediately, so risk is low."
  ],
  "verification_requirements": [
    "After activating the dialog, focus must be on #street input.",
    "Pressing Enter after entering an address must save the address and update the status.",
    "Pressing Escape must close the dialog and return focus to the #open button.",
    "The speed fieldset must remain disabled until an address is saved."
  ]
}

reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The journey steps:

1. Seek "#open", press Enter -> opens dialog (#address-dialog visible)
   Assertion: focus on #street

Observed: After pressing Enter, the dialog opened (visible true), but focus remained on #open (i.e., didn't move to #street). So the issue: When opening the dialog via button click, focus does not move to the first input (#street).

Looking at the script: On click of #open, they set backdrop.hidden = false; trap.activate();

The focus trap likely tries to focus initial focus node based on configuration passed to createFocusTrap.

Let's examine upstream-dependency/focus-trap/index.js usage:

```
const backdrop = document.getElementById('backdrop');
const trap = createFocusTrap('#address-dialog', { onDeactivate: () => { backdrop.hidden = true; } });
document.getElementById('open').addEventListener('click', () => {
  backdrop.hidden = false;
  trap.activate();
});
```

So they call createFocusTrap with element '#address-dialog' and options: only onDeactivate.

Thus default options: returnFocusOnDeactivate: true, escapeDeactivates: true, delayInitialFocus: true (see config defaults in focus-trap). They didn't specify initialFocus or fallbackFocus.

When trap.activate() is called, it will try to focus getInitialFocusNode().

Let's see getInitialFocusNode logic:

```
const getInitialFocusNode = function () {
  let node;

  // false indicates we want no initialFocus at all
  if (getOption({}, 'initialFocus') === false) {
    return false;
  }

  if (getNodeForOption('initialFocus') !== null) {
    node = getNodeForOption('initialFocus');
  } else if (containersContain(doc.activeElement)) {
    node = doc.activeElement;
  } else {
    const firstTabbableGroup = state.tabbableGroups[0];
    const firstTabbableNode =
      firstTabbableGroup && firstTabbableGroup.firstTabbableNode;
    node = firstTabbableNode || getNodeForOption('fallbackFocus');
  }

  if (!node) {
    throw new Error(
      'Your focus-trap needs to have at least one focusable element'
    );
  }

  return node;
};
```

Now, what is state.containers? It is set via trap.updateContainerElements(elements) where elements is the argument passed to createFocusTrap: '#address-dialog' (a string). So containers will be an array containing the element matching that selector.

During activation, before focusing, they call updateTabbableNodes() if checkCanFocusTrap is false (default). Actually in activate method:

```
if (!checkCanFocusTrap) {
  updateTabbableNodes();
}
```

Since they didn't pass checkCanFocusTrap option, it's false, so updateTabbableNodes runs.

updateTabbableNodes builds state.tabbableGroups from containers (the dialog). It uses tabbable(container) to get tabbable nodes within the dialog.

Now, what are tabbable nodes in the dialog? Let's look at the dialog markup:

```
<div class="panel" id="address-dialog" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="address-title">
  <h2 id="address-title">Delivery address</h2>
  <fieldset id="speed-group" disabled>
    <legend>Delivery speed</legend>
    <label for="speed">Available once an address is saved</label><br>
    <select id="speed"><option>Standard</option><option>Express</option></select>
  </fieldset>
  <p><label for="street">Street address</label><br><input id="street" type="text" autocomplete="street-address"></p>
  <button id="save" type="button">Save address</button>
  <button id="cancel" type="button">Cancel</button>
</div>
```

Note: fieldset has disabled attribute, which disables all its contents (including the select). So the select inside disabled fieldset is not focusable/tabbable.

Thus tabbable nodes within dialog: #street input, #save button, #cancel button. Also maybe the h2? No, not tabbable unless tabindex.

Thus first tabbable node should be #street.

But why didn't focus go there? Possibly because the dialog is initially hidden (backdrop hidden) and the trap is activated while the dialog is still hidden? Actually they set backdrop.hidden = false before trap.activate(). So the dialog becomes visible.

But maybe the focus trap's initial focus runs before the DOM updates? However they set backdrop.hidden = false synchronously, then trap.activate() which should run after.

Maybe the issue is that the trap's containersContain(doc.activeElement) check returns true because the active element is still #open button, which is not inside the dialog container (since dialog is not a container of the button). Wait containersContain checks if state.containers.some(container => container.contains(element)). The container is the dialog element. Does dialog contain the button? No, button is outside. So containersContain(doc.activeElement) will be false (since active element is #open button, not inside dialog). Good.

Thus it goes to else: firstTabbableGroup = state.tabbableGroups[0]; firstTabbableNode = group.firstTabbableNode; node = firstTabbableNode || getNodeForOption('fallbackFocus');

If firstTabbableNode is null, then fallbackFocus used. But we expect firstTabbableNode to be #street.

Why would firstTabbableNode be null? Perhaps because the fieldset disabled causes the select to be considered not tabbable, but the tabbable function might still return nodes? Let's check tabbable function in src/index.js (the tabbable library they import). That's the same as focus-trap's dependency? Actually focus-trap imports from 'tabbable' which is the local src/index.js (they have importmap). So the tabbable function is defined in src/index.js.

We need to see if tabbable(container) returns an empty array due to some reason.

Let's examine the tabbable function logic quickly:

It gets candidates via getCandidates(el, includeContainer, filter). filter is isNodeMatchingSelectorTabbable.

isNodeMatchingSelectorTabbable calls isNodeMatchingSelectorFocusable and checks not isNonTabbableRadio and getTabindex >=0.

isNodeMatchingSelectorFocusable checks: node.disabled OR isHiddenInput(node) OR isHidden(node, options.displayCheck) OR isDetailsWithSummary(node) => false.

Thus if node.disabled is true, returns false.

Now, the fieldset is disabled, but the input #street is not disabled. However, note that the fieldset disabled attribute disables its form controls, but does it also set the disabled property on the fieldset element only, not on its children. The spec says that disabled fieldset makes its descendants disabled except for legend. However, the DOM property .disabled on the input will be true? Actually, when a fieldset is disabled, the form controls inside become disabled (their disabled property is true). Let's verify: In HTML, a disabled fieldset makes its descendants disabled, except for <legend>. The disabled attribute is not inherited, but the property .disabled reflects the computed state? I think the input's disabled property will be true when inside a disabled fieldset. Indeed, browsers set the disabled property on form elements inside a disabled fieldset. So #street.disabled will be true, making it not focusable.

Thus tabbable will skip it because isNodeMatchingSelectorFocusable returns false due to node.disabled.

Therefore, there are no tabbable nodes inside the dialog (since the select is also disabled due to fieldset, and buttons? Wait buttons are not inside the fieldset; they are after the fieldset, so they are not disabled. Let's check markup: The fieldset ends before the <p> for street? Actually the HTML:

```
<fieldset id="speed-group" disabled>
  <legend>Delivery speed</legend>
  <label for="speed">Available once an address is saved</label><br>
  <select id="speed"><option>Standard</option><option>Express</option></select>
</fieldset>
<p><label for="street">Street address</label><br><input id="street" type="text" autocomplete="street-address"></p>
<button id="save" type="button">Save address</button>
<button id="cancel" type="button">Cancel</button>
</div>
```

Thus the buttons are after the fieldset, not inside it, so they are not disabled. So they should be tabbable.

Thus tabbable(container) should return at least #save and #cancel buttons. Why would firstTabbableNode be null? Because maybe the tabbable function excludes buttons? No, button is in candidateSelector list.

Let's double-check: candidateSelector includes 'button'. So buttons should be included.

Thus firstTabbableNode should be #save (since it appears before #cancel). Actually order: fieldset, then p with input, then save button, then cancel button. So tabbable nodes: #street input, #save button, #cancel button. The first is #street (if not disabled). But if #street.disabled is true due to fieldset, then it's skipped. Then first tabbable would be #save button.

Thus focus should go to #save button, not remain on #open. Yet observed focus stayed on #open. Something else prevented focusing any node.

Maybe the trap's activation includes delayInitialFocus: true (default). They use delayInitialFocusTimer = config.delayInitialFocus ? delay(function () { tryFocus(getInitialFocusNode()); }) : tryFocus(getInitialFocusNode());

If delayInitialFocus is true, they set a timeout (setTimeout(fn,0)) to tryFocus after the current call stack. However, they also add listeners (focusin, mousedown, etc.) after setting the timer. The timer callback will tryFocus after the call stack clears.

But before that, they also add listeners. The focusin listener (checkFocusIn) may interfere? Let's see.

When the dialog opens, the focus is still on #open button (outside). The trap adds a focusin listener on document (capture:true). When focus changes, the listener runs.

If the trap tries to focus a node via tryFocus after a timeout, but before that, maybe something else steals focus? Not likely.

Alternatively, maybe the trap's activate method does not actually focus because the container is not yet in the DOM? But it is.

Wait: The backdrop is initially hidden (display:none via .backdrop[hidden]{display:none}). They set backdrop.hidden = false (which removes the hidden attribute). However, the backdrop has class "backdrop". The CSS sets .backdrop[hidden]{display:none}. So removing hidden attribute makes it display:flex (since .backdrop has display:flex). So the dialog becomes visible.

But maybe the dialog is still not rendered/layout before the focus trap runs? However, setting attribute triggers reflow synchronously.

Nevertheless, the observed outcome: focus didn't move. Let's see if there is any other code that might reset focus after activation. Not in snippet.

Potential cause: The trap's activate method may be called multiple times? Not.

Let's examine the focus-trap code more thoroughly for any bug that prevents focusing when delayInitialFocus is true and the container is not yet visible? Actually they tryFocus after delay, but they also add listeners before the delay fires. The focusin listener may fire when the button loses focus? Not.

Another possibility: The trap's updateTabbableNodes may be called after activation but before trying to focus, and if it throws an error due to no tabbable nodes and no fallbackFocus, then activation may fail silently? Let's see: In activate, if !checkCanFocusTrap, they call updateTabbableNodes(). If that throws, it would propagate and activation would stop? They don't catch it. If updateTabbableNodes throws, activation would not finish, and focus wouldn't be set. Let's see updateTabbableNodes: It builds state.tabbableGroups from containers using tabbable(container). If tabbable returns empty array, then the group is filtered out (since they filter groups with length>0). Then after mapping/filter, they check:

```
if (
  state.tabbableGroups.length <= 0 &&
  !getNodeForOption('fallbackFocus')
) {
  throw new Error(
    'Your focus-trap must have at least one container with at least one tabbable node in it at all times'
  );
}
```

They didn't provide fallbackFocus option, so getNodeForOption('fallbackFocus') returns null. Thus if state.tabbableGroups.length == 0, they throw.

Thus if tabbable(container) returns empty array, activation will throw error, and focus won't be set. The error might be caught? Not in the code. It would propagate to the click handler and possibly be logged to console, but not shown.

Thus root cause: tabbable(container) returns empty array because all potential tabbable nodes are considered disabled/hidden etc.

We identified that #street is disabled due to being inside a disabled fieldset. The buttons #save and #cancel are not inside fieldset, so they should be tabbable. Why would they be excluded? Let's examine if they are considered disabled due to something else. Maybe they are inside a dialog that is inert? The backdrop may have inert attribute? Not set. The dialog itself may have inert? Not.

But note: The dialog has role="dialog" and aria-modal="true". That shouldn't affect tabbability.

Maybe the tabbable function considers nodes with negative tabindex? Not.

Let's test mentally: For #save button: node.disabled? false. isHiddenInput? no. isHidden(node, options.displayCheck)? options.displayCheck undefined; isHidden uses getComputedStyle(node).visibility === 'hidden'? likely not. isDetailsWithSummary? no. So passes focusable check. Then isNodeMatchingSelectorTabbable: also checks !isNonTabbableRadio(node) (false) and getTabindex(node) >=0. Button's tabIndex default is 0, so passes. So #save should be included.

Thus why tabbable returns empty? Could be because the container element itself is not considered? The getCandidates includes includeContainer flag false by default (they pass includeContainer: false? Actually they call getCandidates(el, options.includeContainer, filter). In updateTabbableNodes they call tabbable(container) with no options, so options is {}; thus includeContainer defaults to false. So they don't include the container itself even if it is tabbable (which it's not because it's a div). So fine.

Thus maybe the issue is that the dialog is not yet in the document when tabbable runs? But they set backdrop.hidden = false before trap.activate(), which should cause the dialog to be rendered. However, the dialog is inside the backdrop div which is initially hidden. Setting backdrop.hidden = false makes the backdrop display:flex, thus the dialog becomes visible. Should be synchronous.

But maybe the browser needs a paint cycle before computing styles for hidden elements? However, getComputedStyle on a hidden element returns computed style as if not rendered? Actually if an element has display:none, getComputedStyle returns values but visibility may be hidden? Not sure. But the dialog is inside a div that had display:none; after changing attribute to remove hidden, the div's display becomes flex (due to .backdrop {display:flex}). So the dialog should be laid out.

Nevertheless, perhaps the tabbable function uses getComputedStyle to check visibility, and if the element is not yet rendered (maybe layout flush hasn't happened), it might still consider it hidden? Unlikely.

Alternative: The fieldset disabled attribute may cause its descendants to be disabled, but also the fieldset itself is disabled, but does that affect the tabbable function's isHidden check? No.

Let's look at the actual tabbable function from src/index.js again to see if there is any other condition that could cause all nodes to be filtered.

We have the source. Let's examine getCandidates:

```
const getCandidates = function (el, includeContainer, filter) {
  let candidates = Array.prototype.slice.apply(
    el.querySelectorAll(candidateSelector)
  );
  if (includeContainer && matches.call(el, candidateSelector)) {
    candidates.unshift(el);
  }
  candidates = candidates.filter(filter);
  return candidates;
};
```

Thus it queries all elements matching candidateSelector within the container (including deeper). candidateSelector includes button, input, select, textarea, a[href], etc. So #save and #cancel buttons should be found.

Then filter = isNodeMatchingSelectorTabbable.bind(null, options). With options undefined.

Thus filter will be isNodeMatchingSelectorTabbable(node, undefined). Options defaults to {}.

Now isNodeMatchingSelectorTabbable:

```
const isNodeMatchingSelectorTabbable = function (options, node) {
  if (
    !isNodeMatchingSelectorFocusable(options, node) ||
    isNonTabbableRadio(node) ||
    getTabindex(node) < 0
  ) {
    return false;
  }
  return true;
};
```

Thus if isNodeMatchingSelectorFocusable returns false, node excluded.

Now isNodeMatchingSelectorFocusable:

```
const isNodeMatchingSelectorFocusable = function (options, node) {
  if (
    node.disabled ||
    isHiddenInput(node) ||
    isHidden(node, options.displayCheck) ||
    /* For a details element with a summary, the summary element gets the focused  */
    isDetailsWithSummary(node)
  ) {
    return false;
  }
  return true;
};
```

Thus if node.disabled is true, excluded.

Now, are the buttons disabled? No.

Thus they should pass.

Thus why would querySelectorAll return empty? Let's double-check the HTML: The buttons are inside the dialog, yes.

But maybe the dialog is not yet in the DOM when the trap is created? The trap is created at module load time, before any user interaction. At that time, the dialog exists in the DOM (it's static HTML). So querySelectorAll should work.

Thus something else.

Let's consider that the tabbable function may rely on the element being displayed (not hidden) to compute offsetParent etc? No, they only use querySelectorAll and matches, not computed styles except in isHidden.

isHidden uses getComputedStyle(node).visibility === 'hidden' OR details not open OR display:none check.

If the node is inside a hidden ancestor (like the backdrop initially hidden), getComputedStyle on the node may still return visibility: visible? Actually visibility is not inherited; if an ancestor has display:none, the node is not rendered but its computed style visibility may still be visible (since visibility defaults to visible). However, the isHidden function also checks display:none via loop up parentNode checking getComputedStyle(parent).display === 'none'. If any ancestor has display:none, it returns true.

Thus while the backdrop is hidden (display:none), the dialog and its children have an ancestor with display:none, so isHidden(node, ...) will return true for all nodes inside, causing them to be filtered out.

Indeed, at the time of trap creation (page load), the backdrop has hidden attribute, so its display is none (due to .backdrop[hidden]{display:none}). Therefore, when tabbable runs during trap.activate()? Wait, updateTabbableNodes is called during activate, after backdrop.hidden = false is set. However, the timing: They set backdrop.hidden = false; then trap.activate(); inside activate, if !checkCanFocusTrap, they call updateTabbableNodes() before adding listeners? Let's look:

```
activate(activateOptions) {
  if (state.active) { return this; }
  const onActivate = getOption(activateOptions, 'onActivate');
  const onPostActivate = getOption(activateOptions, 'onPostActivate');
  const checkCanFocusTrap = getOption(activateOptions, 'checkCanFocusTrap');

  if (!checkCanFocusTrap) {
    updateTabbableNodes();
  }

  state.active = true;
  state.paused = false;
  state.nodeFocusedBeforeActivation = doc.activeElement;

  if (onActivate) { onActivate(); }

  const finishActivation = () => {
    if (checkCanFocusTrap) {
      updateTabbableNodes();
    }
    addListeners();
    if (onPostActivate) { onPostActivate(); }
  };

  if (checkCanFocusTrap) {
    checkCanFocusTrap(state.containers.concat()).then(
      finishActivation,
      finishActivation
    );
    return this;
  }

  finishActivation();
  return this;
}
```

Thus if checkCanFocusTrap is false (default), they call updateTabbableNodes() BEFORE setting state.active = true, before adding listeners, but after the line `const checkCanFocusTrap = ...`. Importantly, they call updateTabbableNodes() before they set backdrop.hidden = false? No, the caller set backdrop.hidden = false before calling trap.activate(). So at the point of updateTabbableNodes(), the backdrop.hidden attribute has been removed (false). However, the CSS rule .backdrop[hidden]{display:none} only applies when the attribute is present. Since they set backdrop.hidden = false, the attribute is removed, so the rule no longer applies, and the backdrop's display becomes flex (due to .backdrop {display:flex}). So the backdrop should not be hidden.

But maybe there is a delay between removing the attribute and the CSS applying? Setting .hidden property should reflect immediately.

Nevertheless, maybe the backdrop still has the hidden attribute because they set .hidden = false but the attribute remains? Actually setting .hidden = false removes the attribute. So it's gone.

Thus at updateTabbableNodes time, the backdrop should not be hidden, and thus isHidden should return false for inner nodes.

But observed failure suggests that updateTabbableNodes still saw them as hidden. Could be because they used the wrong variable: they set backdrop.hidden = false, but the element's id is "backdrop". Yes.

Let's double-check: In the HTML, the backdrop div has id="backdrop" and class="backdrop". Initially it has hidden attribute. In script: const backdrop = document.getElementById('backdrop'); backdrop.hidden = false; That should work.

Thus why would nodes still be considered hidden? Maybe because the dialog itself has inert attribute? Not.

Alternatively, maybe the tabbable function is being called earlier, during trap creation, not during activation. Let's see: createFocusTrap calls trap.updateContainerElements(elements) at the end. That function does:

```
updateContainerElements(containerElements) {
  const elementsAsArray = [].concat(containerElements).filter(Boolean);
  state.containers = elementsAsArray.map((element) =>
    typeof element === 'string' ? doc.querySelector(element) : element
  );

  if (state.active) {
    updateTabbableNodes();
  }

